WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.950 align:start position:0%
 
this presentation is about the breeding  of impatiens royal arana to develop  plants that are resistant  to impatiens downing mildew  before i begin my presentation i'd like  to explain a little bit about long run  about cornell university's campus on  long island  the long island horticulture research  extension center is located on the east  end of long island in the city of  riverhead  we are about 315 miles southeast of the  main campus in ithaca  the research center is located in  suffolk county the leading crop  agricultural county in the state of new  york there is no other county in new  york that is more diverse than suffolk  county  agriculture and horticulture industries  developed in suffolk county  because of the environmental factors  that  are good for plant growth there are  fertile well-drained soils  we are in a unique climatic zone of usda  zone seven  which is considerably warmer than  upstate new york there's plenty of fresh  water and underground aquifers and  plenty of sunlight  with the large markets that are just to  the west of suffolk county  the agriculture community receives very  high returns on their investments  the long island horticulture research  and extension center is one of a kind in  the united states we are the only  horticultural research and extension  center  that has professionals that represent  each of the commodity areas as well as  the cross disciplines  we have specialists in the greenhouse of  floriculture industry  specialists in the food industry with a  specialization in grapes and viticulture  with specialists and vegetables as well  as  woody ornamentals which covers the turf  industry the nursery industry as well as  plant maintenance  addition we have cross-commodity  specialists we have two plant  pathologists  for plant disease  we have a weed scientist we have two  entomologists for insects and bugs and  we have a plant tissue culture  specialist  impatience while ariana  was the most popular bedding plant in  the united states up until about 2011.  it's so popular because it comes in a  variety of colors almost every color  except yellow and a true blue  it's also plant that grows very nicely  in the shade it can even grow in the sun  if it's irrigated properly  the plants are propagated by seed  which is a benefit to the commercial  producers it reduces their costs for  production of this plant and they get a  higher markup  in 2010  the value of impatience in the united  states was 151 million dollars  in the state of new york the value of  impatience was 10.4 million dollars in  sales  but then in patients downing milder hit  the united states and by 2011 the value  of impatience in new york went from 10.4  million down to 1 million dollars  in patients downing mildews of fungal  disease is from the species plasmopera  doosans  it was first reported in the united  states in 2004  it entered the united states in a  hurricane that traveled from africa to  florida  the spores were released in florida and  then the prevailing winds along the east  coast spread this the disease up the  entire eastern coast the united states  by 2011  it is reported across most of the  inhabited world it's globally  distributed  this particular disease can be  controlled by fungicides but the  fungicides that are used are restricted  in the united states and can only be  applied by greenhouse professionals  once the plants leave the greenhouse to  the homeowner's yard  there is no control  that works on this particular disease  this is what happens to a homeowner's  planting of impatience  the photo on the left looks what a nice  planting of impatiens would look like  around the base of a tree  the photo on the right is what they look  like the very same plants after they've  been attacked by impatiens down in  mildew  they lose their leaves they lose their  flowers and all that remains are these  stems  and about three weeks later the entire  plant dies  it's fairly amazing how quickly this  impatiens downing mildew will kill a  plant  it's fairly easy to tell if you have  impatiens down in mildew  turn the leaf  to the underside and look if you see a  white colorations a white fuzzy kind of  colorations that's the spores of the  impatiens down in mildew  the plants the photo on the left  in the forefront show the two plants  that are controls that are filled those  have impatiens down in milden you can  see that they've lost most of their  leaves and their flowers the plants in  the background of that photo are some of  our resistant hybrids  they are still green leafy and quite  healthy looking  once a plant gets in patients down in  mildew there's no cure  and it is a systemic disease it will  never leave the plant  the disease will also remain in the soil  after a plant has been infected so  future years it will re-inoculate new  plants that are planted  the objective of our breeding program  was to use integrasive hybridization  to regenerate resistant plants  we searched for species  of impatients from around the world that  were resistant  to this disease and then cross them with  impatience while we're anna to produce  interspecific hybrids  that would be resistant  you may be familiar with the new guinean  patients which is a different species  that's in patients outcry  they are resistant to a patient's downy  mildew but unfortunately they are not  compatible with impatience while ariana  in 2013 our ornamental plant pathologist  marjorie daughtry  did some preliminary research looking at  ten different  species of impatiens  these this  graph kind of shows you the results of  her research she had five species that  were susceptible to patients down in  mildew and five species that were  resistant  we followed up with this research later  a few years later and we looked at about  40 to 50 different species in our  patients we collected seeds and cuttings  from around the world  grew the plants and then tested them  we set up a screening criteria to  evaluate all of our plants  from zero to five zero being the best  plants and five being the worst plants  if they were ranked with a zero or one  they were  what we considered resistant plants  anything above a one we considered to be  susceptible  these are some of the plants that we  evaluated and we in this particular  table you can see the column on the left  are the species of  that are susceptible to downing milk  the species on the right  are resistant  we use those species in our  hybridization program  to cross within patients while we're  hannah  this just gives you an idea of what we  did  we would take a susceptible species like  the impatiens volariana on the left  and cross it with one of the resistant  species to produce  a hybrid plant  in addition to looking for resistance to  the disease we also evaluated the plants  on their flower color their growth habit  and their ease of growth and propagation  this is another example shown  on the top left  a hybrid of impatiens wallarana  that was crossed with a resistant  species  and you can see in the bottom of the  slide the resulting hybrid which is  quite attractive  during our breeding we used embryo  rescue as a technique to be very helpful  for two main reasons one is that  impatiens have explosive seed pods that  means as the ovary ripens the seed will  explode and scatter the seeds everywhere  by placing  these seeds into  tissue culture we were able to recover  all the seeds that were generated  in addition  by producing seeds in vitro  we were able to have a sterile germplasm  bank for all of our hybrids for future  use  the process is pretty straightforward  we would remove  ovaries about 13 days after pollination  we would take them to our tissue culture  laboratory  and working under the laminar flow hood  we would sterilize them in 95  ethanol and flame them  the ovaries would then be  cut open  and the ovules would be aseptically  removed and placed on a quarter strength  muricious goog medium with only a  quarter strength sugar  they would then be labeled  wrapped with parafilm and placed in the  dark until they germinate which is  anywhere between 15 and 20 days  sometimes a little bit longer  once the seeds germinated we had little  seedlings we would subculture the seeds  the seedlings excuse me onto into  individual culture tubes we would be  able to track them better by placing  them in a tube and giving them an id  number  as the plants grew we would propagate  them and then we would  divide them so that we would have a  clone in vitro and have a clone out in  the greenhouse  the plants in the greenhouse were then  grown until they can be propagated for  us to sufficiently produce enough  replications for the field  at about 2016  we had two hybrids  that were generated that would be  completely resistant to a patient's  downing mildew this was the primary  research of james keach who was getting  his phd with us at the time  we then used these two original plants  to generate everything else that  followed we took these two  lines  we self pollinate them and we cross  pollinate them  and we generated  hundreds of hybrids from these plants  these new hybrids then  were propagated  they were clonally divided so that we  could trial them out in the field and  test them for resistance  and after that  we selected the plants that were  resistant  approximately one-third of our plants  were resistant from these first crosses  the others were susceptible and they  were tossed  following years  2018  we continued to self and cross pollinate  the resistant plants  grew them up in the greenhouse  propagated them and then trialled them  in the field  we discarded anything that was  susceptible  and we we kept the plants  that were resistant  by 2019 we had 30 superior  impatience plants  that were resistant to the downy mildew  we have  all colors of impatience except for a  true white  this gives you an example of what some  of our score sheets look like  we had the number  of the the accession number of the plant  in the left column  we kept track of what the parentage was  of that plant and then their average  score  remember that the lower the number  the better the plant  and anything under the number one was  considered acceptable  anything higher than one was not  acceptable  this shows you how we do some of our  trials we have two hoop houses out in  the field that are covered by 50 shade  we grow the plants in black plastic  with drip tubing underneath the black  plastic  they are grown  outside until  the downy mildew hits usually it's at  the end of august or early september  and then we go and evaluate each plant  once a week  until the plant is dead  for long island we often don't get a  killing frost until the end of october  so  we'll be out there taking data for  an extensive period of time  so we're at the point now  that we're taking these main  plants  and trying to inbreed them to produce  homozygous lines so that eventually we  can produce f1 hybrids  our goal is to produce lines of  impatiens  that can be propagated by seeds  this is our team from this year this  past year they've been working  on this project  we've been very fortunate to have some  great students graduate and  undergraduate students work on this  project  i'll be happy to answer questions later  thank you

